1	\section{Results}
2	\label{sec:results}
3	
4	\subsection{Calibration Quality}
5	\label{sec:calibration_results}
6	
7	\paragraph{Value decomposition.}
8	Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares calibration MAE across methods. Vanilla 2PL without a contamination parameter (B3, $\gamma = 0$) achieves calibration comparable to the full 2PL+$\gamma$ model: ARC MAE 0.016 vs.\ 0.020, GSM8K 0.006 vs.\ 0.006, MMLU 0.007 vs.\ 0.007. The calibration benefit comes from IRT ability estimation itself, which separates stable capability from item-level noise regardless of whether contamination is explicitly modeled. B3 achieves lower ARC MAE than ContamCal because $\gamma$ at $J = 13$ introduces estimation noise. ContamCal nonetheless provides three capabilities beyond vanilla 2PL: $\gamma$ estimates supply domain-level contamination diagnostics (Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}), the posterior yields credible intervals that propagate signal uncertainty, and gating criteria provide principled exclusion rules for benchmarks where $\gamma$ is non-significant.
9	
10	\paragraph{Gating and negative results.}
11	ARC passes gating: MAE reduction of 0.014 relative to B0-Raw (0.034 $\to$ 0.020) and $\gamma$ CI excluding zero ($\tau = 0.756$). GSM8K fails gating ($\gamma$ non-significant). We report two honest negatives: credible interval coverage falls below nominal levels (50\% CI achieves $\sim$35\% empirical coverage), and the GSM8K overcorrection ratio is 11$\times$ due to the non-significant $\gamma$ being applied uniformly.
12	
13	\paragraph{Comparison to baselines.}
14	B0-Raw shows substantial miscalibration on ARC (MAE$=$0.034) but minimal on MMLU (MAE$=$0.016) where diverse domains partially cancel errors. B1-Remove\&Rescore reduces item count without improving calibration. B4-LinearRegression matches IRT on GSM8K but fails on ARC where nonlinear ability-difficulty interaction matters. B5-Emperor \citep{sun2025emperor} confirms that mitigation approaches do not restore clean performance.
15	
16	\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
17	\label{sec:signal_failure}
18	
19	\paragraph{Min-K\%++ directional reversal.}
20	Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items, yielding Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005 and $d = -1.98$ at d100 relative to d000. A practitioner applying Min-K\%++ at this scale would conclude that contaminated items are \textit{less} likely to be memorized. At 1.5B, discrimination turns mildly positive ($d \approx +0.3$); at 3B, moderately positive ($d \approx +0.7$). We hypothesize that SFT injection at 0.5B disrupts the token-level surprisal landscape so severely that contaminated items (overfit at the response level) paradoxically show reduced token-level fluency.
21	
22	\paragraph{Signal reliability across scales.}
23	Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} summarizes detection AUC across signal-scale-dosage combinations. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at d005 and above for all scales. S4 (Self-Critique) performs near-random at 0.5B and reaches marginal discrimination at 1.5B+. RRF fusion degrades to S1-only performance because S2 and S4 contribute noise at small scales.
24	
25	\paragraph{Cross-family signal behavior.}
26	Llama-3.2-3B RRF exposure scores produce valid cross-family IRT fits. Non-significant pooled $\gamma$ values (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) confirm that exposure scores maintain discriminative quality across architectures, but contamination mechanisms differ enough between families that pooled $\gamma$ loses diagnostic specificity.
27	
28	\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
29	\label{sec:identifiability}
30	
31	The simulation study (20 conditions, 10 seeds each, 200 runs) establishes the operating envelope. Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} presents bias and CI coverage as a function of signal quality and ensemble size. Recovery succeeds (bias $< 0.05$, 95\% CI coverage $\geq 90\%$) at AUC as low as 0.55 with $J \geq 50$. Posterior CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$, close to the parametric $J^{-0.5}$ rate. Current IRT-based LLM evaluation operates at $J = 8$--$30$ \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}, well below this threshold. Signal quality is the binding constraint: when AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), even $J = 16$ suffices. Our $J = 13$ results confirm the analysis: calibration via $\theta$ works, but per-domain $\gamma$ remains exploratory.
32	
33	\subsection{Exploratory Domain Analysis}
34	\label{sec:domain_differential}
35	
36	\paragraph{Directional heterogeneity.}
37	Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} and Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma} present per-domain $\gamma$ estimates ($J=13$ Qwen2.5, 1.5B and 3B; 0.5B excluded per \S\ref{sec:injection}). STEM domains show positive $\gamma$ (Math $+0.50$, Science $+0.50$, CS/Eng $+0.50$) while Humanities ($-0.31$) and Social Sciences ($-0.57$) are negative. Only Social Sciences reaches significance ($[-1.05, -0.10]$), consistent with SFT injection on factual QA pairs disrupting contextual reasoning. The pooled MMLU estimate ($\gamma_{\text{pooled}} = +0.11$, CI $[-0.40, +0.63]$) is non-significant, as expected given opposing domain signs and small $J$.
38	
39	\paragraph{Dose-response and cross-family analysis.}
40	Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response} shows monotonically increasing accuracy with dosage for STEM domains, with diminishing returns above d025. Effects are catastrophic at 0.5B (accuracy 0.25 $\to$ 0.60 at d100 for Math) but mild at 3B (3--5 pp increase), consistent with larger models absorbing injected items. Cross-family pooling (Qwen + Llama) yields non-significant $\gamma$ across all benchmarks (MMLU 0.35, GSM8K $-0.13$, ARC 0.01; all CIs include zero) but calibrated MAE remains low (MMLU 0.008, GSM8K 0.008, ARC 0.015), confirming that IRT $\theta$ estimation generalizes across families while $\gamma$ diagnosis is family-specific.
41	
